  1142                              <1> ; =============================================================================
  1143                              <1> ;  Console layer
  1144                              <1> ;
  1145                              <1> ;  putc prints AL. waitk is the host's key routine: it waits up to EAX ms
  1146                              <1> ;  (0: just a look; -1: for ever) for a key, and returns it in AL with the
  1147                              <1> ;  zero flag clear, or AL = 0 with the zero flag set if none came. Both keep
  1148                              <1> ;  every register but EAX (putc keeps EAX too).
  1149                              <1> ;
  1150                              <1> ;  A running program reads the keyboard only through getk and pollk, which
  1151                              <1> ;  between them give the break key its rules:
  1152                              <1> ;    - Ctrl-C stops the program;
  1153                              <1> ;    - any other key that the break check reads is held at HELD for the
  1154                              <1> ;      next '?', and a newer one takes its place;
  1155                              <1> ;    - '?' (getk) takes the held key first;
  1156                              <1> ;    - a program that has stored a non-zero byte at BRKOFF gets Ctrl-C as a
  1157                              <1> ;      key like any other, but two of them within about 250 ms still stop it.
  1158                              <1> ;  Both are called straight from a handler (or from rpoll), never from a
  1159                              <1> ;  routine under one: the checked build's report counts on finding one
  1160                              <1> ;  return address above the data stack.
  1161                              <1> ; =============================================================================
  1162                              <1> 
  1163                              <1> ; putc: WriteFile to the output handle (standard output; the checked
  1164                              <1> ; report switches it to standard error), one byte. PUSHAD saves everything;
  1165                              <1> ; the byte is AL in the saved EAX, and the count goes in the slot of the
  1166                              <1> ; saved ESP, which POPAD ignores.
